SUMMARY:Big Brother & The Holding Company
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261024T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261024T200000
UID:260910ciJpNqUtmkKZbUGrtWRVHA
DESCRIPTION:The band was formed by Peter Albin\, Sam Andrew\, James Gurley
  and Chuck Jones in San Francisco\, in a Victorian mansion/boarding house 
 owned by Peter's uncle at 1090 Page Street in the Haight-Ashbury. That hou
 se became the site of Wednesday night jam sessions which were organized by
  Chet Helms who was the real &quot\;Big Brother\,&quot\; naming the band\,
  bringing James Gurley into the fold and later seeing that his old friend 
 Janis Joplin came to sing with them. The first official Big Brother gig wa
 s at the Open Theater in Berkeley\, January 1966. Within a short time they
  became the house band for Chet at the Avalon Ballroom and began to develo
 p a loyal following\, largely due to the charismatic\, pioneering guitarwo
 rk of James Gurley. The band had what Sam Andrew callled a &quot\;progress
 ive-regressive hurricane blues style\,&quot\; playing such tunes as Hall o
 f the Mountain King\, Coo Coo\, That's How Strong My Love Is\, and Down On
  Me. Janis Joplin made her debut with Big Brother at the Avalon Ballroom i
 n June 1966\, and a year later\, in June 1967\, Big Brother and Janis perf
 ormed at The Monterey Pop Festival. From that point on\, Janis became the 
 main focus of attention\, with her high voltage and raw emotional performa
 nces\, while Big Brother faded into the backgroud and became known for bei
 ng her back-up band.
